## Install

1. Drop `Scripts/{{ s.name }}.cs` into your project's `Assets/`.
2. Add the component to your scene's main Camera.
3. Make sure the contract anchor `{{ s.anchor.transform }}` exists. The tag
   `{{ s.follow_target_tag }}` is retained only as a compatibility fallback.
{% if variant.startswith("cinemachine_") %}
4. The historical `{{ variant }}` label does not require an external
   Cinemachine object; `CameraRig` owns the generated camera contract.
{% endif %}

## Tuning

| Field          | Default | Notes |
|----------------|---------|-------|
| `CameraModeID` | `{{ s.mode_id }}` | stable mode identity |
| `offset`       | ({{ "%.2f"|format(s.spatial.position.base[0]) }}, {{ "%.2f"|format(s.spatial.position.base[1]) }}, {{ "%.2f"|format(s.spatial.position.base[2]) }}) | local-space follow offset |
| `smoothTime`   | {{ "%.3f"|format(s.smooth_time) }} | higher = floatier follow |
| `fovBase`      | {{ "%.1f"|format(s.spatial.fov.base) }} | resting field of view |
| `fovSprint`    | {{ "%.1f"|format(s.fov_sprint) }} | widened FOV during sprint |
| `collision`    | `{{ s.motion.collision.mode }}` | contract collision policy |
| `validation`   | enabled | distance, rotation and FOV checks |
{% if variant == "vehicle_chase" %}

The racing chase variant uses the existing offset as a low trailing mount,
adds a deterministic forward look-ahead, locks the horizon to world up, and
applies only a bounded fraction of the generated vehicle visual lean. FOV and
analytic shake are driven by Rigidbody forward speed; no random state or
additional camera schema fields are required.
{% endif %}
